=== REASONING ===

Why an in-process bounded channel + consumer goroutine
-------------------------------------------------------
Two designs were on the table:

  (a) Direct write — every WriteX method calls w.w.Write + w.flusher.Flush
      synchronously. Simple, no goroutine. But http.ResponseWriter is NOT
      safe for concurrent calls (net/http documents this), so the
      dispatcher and the stall-timeout watcher cannot both call it. The
      stall path also needs to OBSERVE that a write is taking too long
      from outside the call — impossible if the write is a synchronous
      Send on the producer goroutine.

  (b) Channel + consumer goroutine — Send hands an event to a bounded
      chan; a single consumer goroutine drains it and writes to the
      ResponseWriter. Only one goroutine ever touches the writer (the
      consumer); the producer can be cancelled / observed from a
      separate watcher.

Pattern P3 + R4 mandate (b): a single canonical primitive with
back-pressure as a SIGNAL, not a drop. The channel is sized at 64,
which absorbs the typical LLM token-burst (a 7B model emits ~30
tokens/sec; a 64-slot buffer is ~2 seconds of buffer at typical
chunk-per-token granularity — comfortably larger than browser
processing jitter, comfortably smaller than a network stall).

Why blocking-Send + stall-timeout instead of dropping
-----------------------------------------------------
R4 is unambiguous: drops are forbidden. Rationale:

  - SSE delta frames carry partial JSON for tool_call.arguments
    payloads. Dropping a delta corrupts the JSON and the LLM's
    next tool dispatch is invalid.
  - Tool_result frames carry result IDs that downstream confirm
    semantics depend on. Dropping a tool_result orphans a continuation.
  - The frontend has no way to detect a missing frame (SSE has no
    sequence numbers).

So Send blocks. If the consumer can't drain in 5s (default; tunable
via WithStallTimeout), the upstream provider context is cancelled
(stops the LLM mid-generation, freeing both the API quota and the
browser's load), a single terminal {Type: "error", Data:
"stream_stalled"} event is best-effort emitted, and ErrStallTimeout
propagates back to the dispatcher. The dispatcher surfaces this as a
normal failure → frontend banner → R8 baseline fallback.

Why the writer also implements WriteToolResult/Error
----------------------------------------------------
The dispatch.StreamWriter interface omits the tool-call ID from
WriteToolResult and WriteToolError (this is intentional — the
dispatcher's serial execution contract guarantees a result/error
follows directly from the most-recent call). The writer tracks
lastToolCallID inside Send so the SSE frame includes the originating
ID, which the frontend needs to correlate the result with the call
event in the AiStreamEvent union. A unit test
(TestWriteToolResult_PairsWithLastCallID) pins this contract: if the
dispatcher ever loses its serial guarantee, that test breaks loudly.

Why fetch+ReadableStream, not EventSource
-----------------------------------------
EventSource is convenient but only supports GET requests. F5's
streaming endpoint will accept the conversation history + tool
context as a JSON POST body. The fetch/ReadableStream approach is
therefore the only standards-track option; the abort semantics
(AbortController) also map cleanly onto the stall path on the
backend (HTTP request context cancellation propagates as a
normal Send-time error).

Why useAiStream.state remains 'paused-confirm' after the stream closes
----------------------------------------------------------------------
On confirm_request, the server intentionally closes the SSE
connection — the user's decision arrives via a fresh POST to
/api/v1/ai/_internal/continue, which opens a NEW stream. So when
the existing stream's reader returns done, the post-loop fallback
must NOT promote 'paused-confirm' to 'done', or the SPA dialog
prematurely transitions out of confirm mode. The fallback only
flips 'streaming' → 'done'. A unit test
("moves to paused-confirm on confirm_request") pins this contract.

Why the contract test is text-level instead of AST-level
--------------------------------------------------------
SSE has no schema language. The Go writer types and the TS hook
types are independent declarations of the same logical contract.
A text-level scan of writer.go for json:"<field>" tags + a
text-level scan of useAiStream.ts for property references is enough
to catch the ONE failure mode that matters: a field added to one
side and forgotten on the other. An AST walker would add maintenance
cost without catching anything more — the Go side is type-checked
by the compiler; the TS side is type-checked by tsc; the contract
test catches drift BETWEEN them. If the contract grows (e.g. nested
payloads) the right next step is JSON-Schema codegen, not a more
complex linter.

ADR-015 alignment
-----------------
F5 ships infrastructure that COULD be reached by an AI request, but
it is not wired into any router this slice modifies. Default-off
remains intact (the writer is constructed only by handlers behind
guard.Wrap). The non-AI baseline (ChatbotPage typewriter) is
unmodified by this slice. Stall observability (I12) is implemented
with the new metrics; cardinality is bounded by the registry
(currently 3 features → 3 label values).

=== CHANGES ===

Production code (new):

  internal/ai/stream/doc.go               (43 lines)
      Package doc explaining R4 back-pressure semantics, the
      channel/consumer architecture, the stall-timeout decision,
      and the ADR-015 alignment.

  internal/ai/stream/writer.go            (562 lines)
      The SSE Writer. Constructor: New(ctx, w, featureID, opts...).
      Implements dispatch.StreamWriter (compile-time asserted). Adds
      WriteConfirmRequest, WriteDoneFull, WriteError, Close, Wait.
      Bounded chan(64), single consumer goroutine, stall-timeout
      with upstream ctx cancel + best-effort error frame, lastToolCallID
      tracking for tool_result/tool_error pairing. Five Prometheus
      metrics, all labeled by feature_id.

  web/src/hooks/useAiStream.ts            (404 lines)
      React hook returning { start, cancel, state, text, error }.
      Built on fetch + ReadableStream + TextDecoder; SSE frames split
      on \r?\n\r?\n. AbortController for cancellation; cleanup on
      unmount. Exports parseSSEFrame and toTypedEvent for reuse.
      State machine: idle → streaming → (done | paused-confirm | error).
      paused-confirm is preserved across stream close (server intentionally
      closes after confirm_request; SPA opens a fresh stream against
      /continue with the continuation_id).

Tests (new):

  internal/ai/stream/writer_test.go       (577 lines, 15 tests)
      Coverage: header emission, every event type, empty-delta no-op,
      tool_call → tool_result ID pairing, tool_error → ok:false,
      confirm_request, done with usage, terminal error then further
      sends fail, idempotent close, stall-timeout cancels upstream
      ctx + emits stall frame, send-after-stall returns ErrWriterClosed,
      consumer-write-failure path, feature_id label propagation,
      compile-time interface assertion against dispatch.StreamWriter,
      and a non-flushable rejection test using a hand-rolled stub
      (httptest.ResponseRecorder DOES implement Flush in modern Go).

  web/src/hooks/__tests__/useAiStream.test.ts   (328 lines, 19 tests)
      Coverage: parseSSEFrame on valid + malformed input, hook
      transitions through idle/streaming/done, delta accumulation,
      tool_call/tool_result event passthrough, confirm_request →
      paused-confirm AND remains in paused-confirm after stream close,
      explicit error event → state=error, network failure → state=error,
      404 surface → error='stream_http_404', cancel propagates to
      AbortController, unmount cleanup.

Tooling (new):

  tools/aistream-contract/main.go         (239 lines)
      Standalone Go program. Reads internal/ai/stream/writer.go and
      web/src/hooks/useAiStream.ts as text. Asserts every event type
      literal (delta / tool_call / tool_result / confirm_request /
      done / error) appears on both sides AND every JSON field name
      from the canonical EXPECTED_FIELDS map appears on both sides.
