I6 (404 routes preserved):
  PASS — Zero new HTTP routes added. aivet reports the same 5 AI
         route count as the F4 baseline. The streaming endpoint that
         WILL eventually call stream.New is a future U-slice
         responsibility; F5 ships only the in-process plumbing.
  EVIDENCE: aivet output: "OK — 5 AI route(s), 3 feature(s) in
            registry, TS mirror in sync".

I12 (stall observability — INTRODUCED by F5):
  PASS — The stall path is fully observable:
           1. teslasync_ai_stream_stall_total{feature_id} increments
              once per stall.
           2. The upstream provider context is cancelled (so the LLM
              call stops billing immediately).
           3. A best-effort terminal {Type: "error", Data:
              "stream_stalled"} SSE frame is emitted (browser sees
              a real failure, not a silent hang).
           4. ErrStallTimeout propagates back to the caller for
              error-rate metrics + log correlation.
           5. teslasync_ai_stream_duration_seconds records the
              actual elapsed time so dashboards distinguish "fast
              ok" from "slow ok" from "stalled".
         No drop counter exists by design — drops are forbidden by
         R4. Cardinality is bounded by the feature registry
         (currently 3 → 3 label values, well under any practical
         Prometheus limit).
  EVIDENCE: writer_test.go::TestStallTimeout_CancelsCtxAndEmitsStallFrame
            asserts (a) the upstream context returns ctx.Err()
            non-nil after the stall fires, (b) the response body
            contains "stream_stalled", (c) Send returns
            ErrStallTimeout. writer_test.go::TestFeatureIDIsRecorded
            asserts the metric is labeled correctly. No drop COUNTER
            metric is registered (the metric registry block in
            writer.go declares only open_total, chunk_total,
            stall_total, cancel_total, and duration_seconds — there
            is no streamDropTotal collector by design).

R4 (back-pressure as signal, not drop):
  PASS — The bounded channel (cap=64) is the only buffer for normal
         events. When full, Send BLOCKS the producer (the channel
         send in the Send method's select has no `default:` branch
         on the data path — only timer.C triggers the stall, which
         is a terminal observable failure with metrics + ctx
         cancellation, NOT a silent drop). The consumer goroutine
         is a `for fr := range w.ch` loop with no select-default
         either; it cannot drop frames mid-stream. The stall
         watcher is a separate, observable terminal failure mode —
         silent drops on the data path are impossible by
         construction.
  EVIDENCE: writer.go::Send (L207-235) — the only `default:` clause
            in this method is in the BEST-EFFORT TERMINAL stall
            error frame at L228-231 (after streamStallTotal has
            already incremented and the upstream ctx has been
            cancelled — the producer will receive ErrStallTimeout
            regardless). writer.go::consume (L399-417) — `for fr :=
            range w.ch` with no select. The drainAfterFailure
            "drop" comment at L425 runs ONLY after writeFrame has
            returned an error, drainErr has been stored, and the
            HTTP transport is already broken — every subsequent
            Send observes the drain error and returns it instead
            of accepting more events. So a drop on the normal
            event path is unreachable.

I8 (audit survives downgrade):
  N/A — F5 writes nothing to durable storage. Stream events are
        emitted live to an HTTP response and discarded after the
        request completes. The dispatcher (F4) is responsible for
        ai_call_log persistence; F5 only carries bytes between
        producer and ResponseWriter.

I9 (provenance visible):
